WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.400
Stan Lee is a pop culture titan.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.660
With a career that spans eight decades, he’s
been around long enough to see characters

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.620
he co-created become household names, a roster
that includes Spider-Man, the X-Men, the Hulk,

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.120
and more.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.980
His journey has been remarkable, but it might
not be what you’d expect from the smiling

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.540
spokesman for Marvel Comics.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:18.980
Stan “The Delivery Man” Lieber

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.120
Lee was born Stanley Martin Lieber, the son
of Jewish immigrants from Romania.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.230
As a student, he worked odd jobs to help his
family get through the tail end of the Great

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.410
Depression, including delivering sandwiches
in Manhattan, a far cry from his dreams of

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.580
writing sci-fi novels.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.260
Not long after graduating high school at 16,
however, he got a job at a company run by

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.560
one of his relatives, Martin Goodman... a
little place called Timely Comics.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.540
Stan Lieber No More!

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.820
When he joined up with Timely Comics in 1939,
Stan Lieber thought that his job as an errand

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.360
boy would be a stepping stone to a career
as a novelist.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.340
Needless to say, things turned out a little
differently.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.539
In 1941, Stan was assigned to write a text-only
story as filler for Captain America Comics

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.220
#3.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.339
For this story, "Captain America Foils the
Traitor's Revenge," he used the pseudonym

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.940
Stan Lee, intending to save his real name
for his future career as a serious novelist.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.640
Within months, the imaginative new kid was
off creating superheroes of his own, with

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.780
names like Jack Frost and Father Time.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.719
Just before Lee turned 19, Captain America’s
creators, Joe Simon and Jack Kirby, left over

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.600
a dispute with Goodman, and Lee was named
interim editor.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.680
Before his career could really take off, though,
World War II would force him into a brief

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.180
detour.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.020
Soldier Stan

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.360
Stan Lee joined the U.S. Army in 1942, where
his talent with words led to assignments writing

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.660
training manuals and slogans for posters.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.900
Before long, he ended up in the Army's Training
Film Division writing scripts, and, as he

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:46.159
would later recount, he was one of only nine
people in the entire Army whose title was

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.300
listed as "playwright."

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.180
While serving in the army, Stan continued
to send in scripts for Timely’s popular

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:55.909
titles, and when the war ended, Lee, only
23 years old, reclaimed the job as editor,

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:57.760
and wouldn't give it up for decades.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.400
But not before thinking about quitting entirely...

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.480
Reinventing Superheroes

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:07.039
By the 1950s, controversy over the content
in comic books had been raging for years,

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:11.120
to the point where publishers like MAD Magazine’s
Bill Gaines were called to testify before

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:11.620
the senate.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.900
The result was the Comics Code, a strict set
of regulations that put several publishers

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.060
out of business, and limited the kinds of
stories creators could tell.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.840
Faced with declining sales, a lack of respect
for comics, and the restrictions of the Code,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.560
Lee was demoralized enough to quit.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.680
Fortunately, his wife Joan convinced him to
try something new.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.140
Inspired by their competition’s recent success
with putting their biggest heroes into a team

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.709
book called Justice League of America, Stan
formed a team of is own, partnering with Jack

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:39.560
Kirby to create the Fantastic Four.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.379
In November of 1961, the rebranded Marvel
Comics launched with Fantastic Four #1, introducing

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.820
readers to a superhero team that combined
sci-fi origins with relatable personalities.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.860
In between battling supervillains and interdimensional
threats, the team bickered, fought, and acted

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.800
like you'd expect regular, flawed people with
superpowers to act.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.030
The immediate success led Goodman to order
basically as many new characters and comic

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.120
books as Lee, Kirby, and the other Marvel
artists could come up with.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.200
Before long, the Marvel Bullpen was known
to the world, with Stan “The Man” Lee’s

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.660
jovial editorials making him the friendly
face of the Marvel Age of Comics.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:14.940
Amazing Fantasies

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.459
As a kid, Stan Lee had been a fan of a pulp
magazine character called the Spider, which

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.760
gave him the idea to approach Goodman about
introducing a teen character with spider-themed

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.260
powers.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.800
Lee would later recall that this idea was
met with some pretty big skepticism from Goodman.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.599
“Nobody wanted me to do it!

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.300
My publisher, when I suggested the idea, he
said, ‘That’s the worse thing I ever heard

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:34.800
of.’”

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:35.400
“Why?”

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.200
“People hate spiders!”

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.360
Rather than risking a whole new series on
the wild idea, Lee instead decided to try

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.300
his character out in Amazing Fantasy, a series
that was due to be canceled anyway.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.400
With Kirby proving to be a bad fit for the
character, artist Steve Ditko took a crack

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.880
at it instead, and the world was introduced
to Peter Parker, the Amazing Spider-Man.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.680
After Amazing Fantasy #15 went flying off
the shelves, Spidey was quickly given his

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.340
own series, paving the way for another wave
of Marvel heroes.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.100
Marvel hits Hollywood

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.080
By the 21st century, the Marvel Cinematic
Universe would be breaking box office records,

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:11.290
but Marvel’s, and Stan’s, original road
to Hollywood was actually a lot more difficult

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.260
than they expected.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.460
In the '70 s, there were plenty of attempts
at bringing Marvel properties to television.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:21.690
The Amazing Spider-Man debuted on CBS as a
TV movie in 1977, and while the technical

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.880
limitations were obvious, fans seemed thrilled
to have a live-action version of the webslinger

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:26.960
airing in prime time.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:28.380
It didn’t last, though.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.220
CBS pulled the plug after 13 episodes because
of its more successful Marvel adaptation,

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.000
The Incredible Hulk.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.330
Featuring Bill Bixby as Dr. Banner and Lou
Ferrigno as his rampaging alter ego, The Incredible

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.700
Hulk aired for five seasons, and helped to
cement the character in the pop culture consciousness.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.290
Unfortunately, the attempts at launching Doctor
Strange and Captain America through TV movies

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.940
didn’t work out, and Marvel’s hard luck
got worse when the entire comics industry

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.180
hit a slump in the ‘90s.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.010
Through it all, though, Stan continued to
be an ambassador for the brand, and while

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:01.060
it had been years since he’d actually been
an editor at Marvel, a new wave of Marvel

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.760
films in 2000 led to what has today become
his signature: the well-placed cameo.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.980
“Superheroes in New York?

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.440
Gimme a break.”

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.260
The face of Marvel

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:16.160
Since the beginning of Marvel's rise to pop
culture dominance over 50 years ago, Stan

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.280
Lee's face and personality have only grown
more associated with the brand.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:24.500
He's lent his voice to video games and cartoons,
appeared in almost every Marvel movie, and

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.080
even writes the occasional new comics story
for Marvel.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.020
It all became possible because a creative
kid with an illustrious background in sandwich

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.500
delivery and a struggling young industry took
a chance on each other, and because of Joan

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.980
Lee, the greatest advice-giver there ever
was.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.380
Excelsior!

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:44.020
“You know, I guess one person can make a
difference.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:47.240
Nuff said.”

